Durarara!! AU
Chapter 11 – Part 1
After the final bell, the four of them ended up near the school gates without anyone fully deciding on it.
Anri had simply looked at Curtis when Masaomi and Mikado started walking with her, a quiet, unspoken invitation. He had fallen into step without comment, long black braids shifting against the blue uniform jacket, mismatched eyes half-lidded and unreadable. By the time they stopped under the partial shade of the trees outside the main gate, he was part of the circle whether he had planned to be or not.
Masaomi was the one who brought it up.
“There’s something you should know about Nasujima,” he said, glancing once at Anri before including the other two. “He used to date a second-year named Haruna Niekawa. She transferred out of Raira not long after it ended. The timing always felt off to me.”
Mikado frowned, filing the name away. Curtis remained silent, listening.
The conversation shifted, as it often did when the three original members of the group were together. Masaomi and Mikado started asking—carefully, but directly—about the girls who had been giving Anri trouble, and about Mika Harima’s continued absence. The questions were familiar. Concern wrapped in the awkwardness of people who still didn’t know how hard to push.
Anri answered the way she always did: soft, precise, and a little distant from her own history.
“In middle school I used Mika for protection,” she said. “She was popular enough that people left me alone if I stayed near her. And Mika used me to make herself look better—kinder, more patient than she actually was. It worked for both of us until it didn’t.”
A small pause.
“She’s been gone for a while now. I don’t know where.”
Mikado immediately stepped in, the same earnest defense he always offered her.
“There’s nothing wrong with wanting to be better than the people around you, or with taking the class rep position. It’s normal. You didn’t do anything wrong by accepting it.”
Anri’s gaze moved from Mikado to Masaomi, then settled—quietly, deliberately—on Curtis.
She waited.
The newest member of the circle had been silent the entire time. Now the space was open for whatever he would, or wouldn’t, say.
Curtis had been quiet through the entire exchange.
When Anri’s eyes settled on him, he didn’t rush the answer. He simply looked at her for a moment, mismatched green and blue eyes steady, then spoke in the same even voice he used for most important things.
“Anri… you weren’t using Mika. You were surviving.”
The words landed cleanly in the small circle.
“And wanting to grow past who you were back then isn’t selfish—it’s healing. You’re allowed to become someone stronger without feeling guilty about it.”
He continued without raising his voice or looking away.
“You don’t owe your past self anything. You don’t owe Mika anything. Becoming class rep doesn’t mean you’re trying to be ‘better’ than her—it means you’re finally choosing something for yourself. And I think that’s brave.”
A short pause. The afternoon light shifted through the trees above them.
“You always assume you’re the one doing something wrong… but you weren’t. Mika wasn’t protecting you—she was using you. You’re allowed to step out of that shadow. Being class rep is you proving to yourself that you can stand on your own.”
Silence followed.
Mikado stared at Curtis like the transfer student had just said something he had been trying (and failing) to articulate for weeks. Masaomi’s usual sharp expression had gone thoughtful, the edge of his usual protectiveness softened by reluctant agreement.
Anri’s fingers tightened slightly around the strap of her bag. For a moment she didn’t speak. The soft, careful composure she wore like armor had thinned, just enough to show something more fragile underneath.
She looked at Curtis as if she were still deciding whether the words were allowed to stay.
Anri was quiet for a long moment.
The afternoon noise of students leaving campus moved around their small group, but none of it seemed to reach her right away. She kept her eyes on the ground near Curtis’s shoes, long enough that Mikado started to look worried and Masaomi shifted his weight like he was preparing to smooth something over.
Then she spoke, voice softer than usual.
“…No one has ever said it like that.”
She finally looked up, meeting the mismatched eyes that had delivered the words without pity or performance.
“I’ve always thought of it as something I did wrong. Using her. Letting her use me. Taking the class rep position felt like… proof that I was still doing the same thing—trying to stand somewhere higher so I wouldn’t get stepped on.” A small, almost fragile breath. “You make it sound like it was allowed.”
Her fingers loosened slightly on her bag strap.
“Thank you, Curtis-kun.”
It was simple. No long explanation. No deflection. Just the quiet acceptance of someone who had been carrying a particular kind of guilt for years and had just been told—clearly, without conditions—that she could set it down.
She didn’t look away from him this time.
The conversation didn’t stay soft for long.
Mikado, still turning over everything that had been said about Mika and the gaps in what they knew, looked between them with that familiar earnest spark.
“What if we used the Dollars?” he suggested. “They’re all over the city. Someone has to have seen Mika, or Seiji. We could put the word out and—”
“No.”
Masaomi’s voice cut in sharper than before. The easy posture he usually carried around Mikado had gone tight.
“You’re not getting involved with that. The Dollars aren’t a missing-persons service, and Ikebukuro’s darker than you think. I’ve already told you—I’m keeping you away from that side of the city.”
The finality in it left little room for argument. Mikado opened his mouth, then closed it again, the protest dying under the look Masaomi gave him.
A short, awkward silence settled over the group.
Masaomi exhaled through his nose, then glanced at Curtis—who had remained quiet through the exchange, long black braids still against the blue uniform, expression unreadable.
“…You heading out?” Masaomi asked him. “I’ll walk with you.”
Curtis gave a small shrug, already shifting his weight to leave.
“Sure.”
He didn’t make a production of it. He simply nodded once to Anri, then to Mikado, and fell into step with Masaomi as the blond started down the path away from the gates.
Mikado turned toward Anri, the offer to walk her part of the way already forming—
—but she was already moving.
Anri adjusted the strap of her bag, gave Mikado a soft, polite “I’ll see you tomorrow,” and started in the opposite direction. There was a quiet difference in the way she carried herself after Curtis’s words earlier—something a little lighter, a little less folded inward. She didn’t look back.
Mikado stood alone near the gates as both pairs of people walked away from him.
The frustration from earlier—watching Curtis step in with Nasujima, hearing him say the exact things Anri had needed, then watching her leave still carrying the weight of those words—settled deeper this time. It wasn’t loud. It didn’t have a clear shape yet. But it was growing, feeding on every moment he had been a step too slow, a sentence too late, or simply not the person she had looked at when she needed something solid.
He tightened his grip on his bag and started the walk home by himself, the name Curtis Nightstar sitting uncomfortably in the middle of his thoughts.
They walked a full block before Masaomi spoke again.
The usual edge he carried in group settings had thinned into something more practical, more tired.
“Hey. Nightstar.” He kept his eyes on the street ahead. “I need to ask you something.”
Curtis glanced sideways, mismatched eyes half-lidded under the afternoon light.
“Yeah?”
Masaomi exhaled through his nose.
“Could you help look after Mikado?”
Curtis’s brow furrowed slightly.
“Why?”
“Because you already move through this city like you’ve been here longer than the rest of us,” Masaomi said, voice low and direct. “You don’t flinch at gangs, you don’t freeze when things get ugly, and you don’t need someone to explain how dark Ikebukuro can get. Mikado’s not like that. He’s a small-town kid. This is his first time living in a place like this. He still thinks the Dollars are some kind of adventure and that asking the wrong questions won’t get him hurt.”
A short, frustrated pause.
“I’ve been trying to keep him away from the worst of it, but he doesn’t always listen to me. You, though… he watches you. Whether he likes it or not. If you’re around, maybe he’ll think twice before walking straight into something he can’t handle.”
Curtis was quiet for a few steps, long black braids shifting against the blue uniform jacket as he considered the request.
“…You’re asking me to watch him because I don’t scare easy,” he said eventually. “Not because we’re friends.”
It wasn’t an accusation. Just a clarification.
Masaomi gave a short, humorless huff.
“I’m asking because I’d rather he stay in one piece. If that means leaning on the transfer student who cracks concrete and tells off teachers without blinking, then yeah. That’s what I’m doing.” He glanced at Curtis. “You in or not?”
Curtis kept walking, expression unchanged.
“Hard to say.” His voice stayed even. “Last time we really spoke, I was on the tail end of a trash can with that rat Izaya. Not to mention you and I didn’t exactly get off to the right start.”
A short pause. The long black braids shifted as he glanced sideways at Masaomi.
“Oddly enough, I didn’t even introduce myself to Mikado properly. Anri pulled me along today.”
He wasn’t refusing. He was laying the complications out in the open—the leftover tension from the street fight, the lack of any real foundation with Mikado, the fact that he had been absorbed into the group by proximity rather than choice.
Masaomi was quiet for a few steps, jaw working like he was chewing over the accuracy of every point.
“…Yeah,” he admitted finally. “That’s fair.”
He shoved his hands into his pockets, the usual sharp confidence tempered by something closer to reluctant honesty.
“I’m not asking you to be his friend. And I’m not pretending you and I are good. You called me a flirty pig and I punched you in the face. We’re not starting from a clean slate.” A dry, humorless breath. “But Mikado still looks at this city like it’s a story he’s allowed to walk into. You already know better. That’s the only reason I’m asking.”
He glanced at Curtis again.
“You don’t have to decide right now. Just… don’t ignore it if you see him about to do something stupid.”
Curtis let out a short sigh, long black braids shifting as he adjusted the strap of his bag.
“I’ll do what I can.”
He left the agreement there for a moment, then kept talking, voice still even and observational.
“By the way. You looked uncomfortable around Izaya. Not to mention you flipped when Mikado mentioned the Dollars. You’re asking me to help keep an eye on him.” A small pause. “With all these factors… were you some sort of gang member?”
Curtis glanced sideways at him, mismatched eyes calm but sharp.
“No. Despite your carefree attitude and the serious nature underneath… I’m guessing a leader of a small gang.”
The accuracy of it sat heavy in the air between them.
Masaomi’s steps slowed for half a second. The easy, sharp-edged mask he usually wore in public thinned, just enough to show the calculation underneath. He didn’t deny it outright. He also didn’t look surprised that the transfer student had put it together so cleanly.
“…You’re dangerous when you pay attention,” Masaomi muttered.
He looked ahead again, jaw tight.
“Yeah. Something like that. A while ago. It’s done now—or it’s supposed to be. But the city doesn’t forget that easy, and neither do the people who used to follow you.” A short, bitter breath. “Mikado doesn’t need to find out how deep that goes. Not if I can help it.”
He glanced at Curtis once more, the earlier request now carrying a heavier context.
“So when I say keep an eye on him… that’s what I’m talking about. Not just street fights and bad ideas. The kind of history that pulls people back in whether they want it or not.”
Masaomi kept walking, but something in his chest had gone cold.
He had asked for help. He had laid out just enough of the truth to make the request make sense. And in return, Curtis Nightstar had looked at the gaps, the reactions, the history Masaomi hadn’t named—and put it together with the same clean, unsettling precision Izaya used when he decided someone was interesting.
He pays attention.
He’s smart.
The way he picked me apart just now… that wasn’t a lucky guess.
A small, sharp part of Masaomi’s mind whispered that he might have made a mistake.
He had invited someone into the quiet responsibility of watching Mikado because that someone didn’t scare easily and already moved through Ikebukuro’s uglier edges without flinching. But the same qualities that made Curtis useful also made him dangerous in a different way. People who could read a former gang leader from a few tells and a short conversation were the kind of people who collected leverage without even trying.
He’s not Izaya.
He doesn’t smile like that. He doesn’t treat people like toys.
But the comparison still sat there, cold and unwanted, as they continued down the street.
Masaomi shoved his hands deeper into his pockets and didn’t look at Curtis again right away. The request still stood. The need to keep Mikado away from the old darkness still mattered more than the discomfort of being seen so clearly.
He just couldn’t shake the feeling that he had handed a very sharp knife to someone who already knew how to use one.
Masaomi walked another half block in silence before he spoke again.
When he did, the casual edge was gone. The question came out flat and deliberate—the kind of thing he only asked when he needed the truth more than he needed to stay comfortable.
“Be honest with me.”
He kept his eyes on the street ahead, but every bit of his attention was fixed on the tall transfer student beside him.
“You’re not just some guy who showed up and got good at reading people by accident. The way you moved in that fight, the way you put me together just now, the way you already had Anri trusting you enough to pull you into the group…” A short, tight pause. “What are you actually doing in Ikebukuro, Nightstar?”
He finally glanced sideways, expression hard.
“Because if you’re the kind of person who collects people the same way Izaya does, I need to know that now—before I let you anywhere near Mikado.”
Curtis’s expression didn’t change, but his voice cooled.
“Then you shouldn’t confide in people half-cocked.” He kept walking, mismatched eyes forward. “The way you just got defensive just now—your body language is the same as when Izaya was near you. You don’t trust me now.”
A short, flat pause.
“Well, I’m here to finish a list me and my frie—”
He stopped mid-word.
One hand went to the inside pocket of the blue uniform jacket, then to the other side, then patted down the front with increasing sharpness. The calm he had carried through the entire conversation fractured into something tighter and more urgent.
“What the fuck—where is it?!”
Masaomi slowed, thrown by the sudden shift.
One second the transfer student had been dissecting him with unnerving accuracy. The next he was searching his own clothes like something critical had vanished. The sharp, controlled presence Curtis usually projected cracked just enough to show real agitation underneath.
“…Nightstar?” Masaomi’s voice carried a new edge of caution. “What are you talking about? What went missing?”
He didn’t step closer. The same instinct that kept him wary of Izaya kept him at a measuring distance now, watching the tall foreigner search his pockets a second time with growing frustration. Whatever Curtis had just lost, it mattered enough to derail the entire conversation—and that alone made Masaomi’s earlier unease settle deeper.
Curtis stopped walking entirely.
His hand stayed pressed against the empty inside pocket as the realization landed in pieces.
“What the hell.” His voice had gone tight. “Did that rat take it when he stabbed me? No…” He exhaled sharply through his nose, mismatched eyes narrowing. “Then I dropped it at that weird dude’s apartment. Fuck.”
Masaomi stared at him.
The casual walk had fully derailed. One second they had been circling trust and gang history. The next, Curtis Nightstar was standing in the middle of the sidewalk looking genuinely rattled over something missing—and casually referencing both getting stabbed by Izaya and some “weird dude’s” apartment in the same breath.
“…Okay. Slow down.” Masaomi’s tone had shifted into something more serious. “You’re telling me Izaya stabbed you, and now you’ve lost something important enough to stop mid-conversation over it?”
He studied Curtis’s face, the earlier wariness mixing with sharp curiosity.
“What exactly did you drop? And what ‘weird dude’ are you talking about?”
Masaomi had never been to Shinra’s place. The name wouldn’t mean anything to him yet. But the combination of Izaya, a stabbing, and Curtis looking actually shaken was enough to put him fully on edge.
Masaomi watched the tall transfer student search his pockets one more time, the earlier cold suspicion in his chest loosening despite himself.
Curtis was sharp. The way he had dissected Masaomi’s reactions, the body language, the history—that was the same kind of clean, uncomfortable intelligence Izaya used when he decided someone was worth taking apart. For a moment it had been enough to make Masaomi regret asking for help at all.
But this was different.
Izaya never looked shaken over a lost photograph. Izaya never cut himself off mid-sentence because something personal had gone missing. Izaya never carried the kind of quiet, frustrated care that was written across Curtis’s face right now. Whatever had been in that pocket mattered. And the fact that it mattered—that Curtis clearly cared—pulled some of the worst of Masaomi’s comparisons apart.
He’s smart like that flea.
But he’s not empty the same way.
Curtis was still standing there, jaw tight, clearly replaying the last places he had been. The unfinished sentence from earlier still hung between them: I’m here to finish a list me and my frie—
Masaomi exhaled slowly and adjusted his tone.
“…Hey.” He kept his voice lower, less confrontational than before. “Before you completely spiral—you were about to tell me what you’re actually doing in Ikebukuro. Something about a list and a friend.”
A short pause.
“You still want to finish that sentence, or are we dealing with the missing thing first?”
Curtis’s expression closed off again, the earlier agitation sealed back under control.
“Forget it.” His voice had returned to that flat, even register. “I’m here to finish a list. That is all. On that list is college here in Japan. Then detective work.”
He didn’t offer more. No explanation of the friend he had almost named. No details about what kind of detective work. Just the clean, limited version—enough to answer the question without opening the parts he had already decided to keep shut.
Masaomi studied him in silence for a second.
Of course.
That’s why he’s sharp like Izaya.
The observational precision, the way he read body language and history from almost nothing, the calm dissection of people in real time—it tracked. Someone training themselves toward detective work would notice the things most people walked past. It didn’t erase the earlier comparison completely, but it gave the sharpness a direction that wasn’t pure manipulation.
“…Detective work,” Masaomi repeated, testing the words. “That’s a hell of a thing to put on a list after college.”
He didn’t push for the rest of the unfinished sentence about the friend. The wall had already come back up, and Masaomi knew better than to hammer on a door someone had just locked. Still, the new information settled into place alongside everything else he had observed about the tall foreigner with the braids and the mismatched eyes.
College.
A list.
Detective work.
And something important enough to lose that had clearly belonged to someone else.
They had been walking in a heavier silence for a few minutes when Masaomi’s steps slowed, then stopped entirely.
Curtis followed his gaze upward.
Raira General Hospital rose against the late afternoon sky. On one of the upper floors, a girl sat in a hospital bed near the window, looking out over the street. Even from this distance the quiet stillness of her posture was clear. She didn’t wave. She simply watched the world she couldn’t currently walk through.
Masaomi stood there with his hands in his pockets, the sharp edges he usually carried nowhere in sight. Whatever mask he wore for Mikado, for the school, for most of Ikebukuro—it had thinned here.
Curtis watched him for a second, then spoke, voice low and even.
“You look upset. You good, man?”
Masaomi didn’t answer right away. His eyes stayed fixed on the window.
“…Yeah.” The word came out quieter than his usual tone. “I’m good.”
A short pause. He didn’t look away from the girl in the bed.
“That’s Saki. She’s… someone important to me. Been in there a while.” His jaw tightened almost imperceptibly. “Some things that happened before you showed up in this city. Things I’m still dealing with.”
He finally glanced sideways at Curtis, the earlier wariness still present but mixed with something more raw.
“You don’t need the whole story. Just—don’t mention this to Mikado. He doesn’t know how bad it got.”
Curtis nodded once, the offer simple and without pressure.
“I respect you telling me this. I won’t say nothing, man.” He glanced from the upper window back to Masaomi. “You wanna keep moving, or are you going to head in? I can wait out here.”
Masaomi shook his head.
The movement was small but final. He kept his eyes on the window for another second, then deliberately turned away from the hospital, shoving his hands deeper into his pockets.
“…Not today.” His voice had gone quieter again. “She doesn’t need me standing down here looking like that. And I don’t need to walk in there with my head still full of everything else.”
He started walking again, slower than before, clearly choosing a route that put the hospital behind them.
“Thanks for not pushing it.” A short, rough breath. “Most people either ask too many questions or pretend they didn’t see anything. You just… offered to wait. That’s rarer than it should be.”
He didn’t look at Curtis as he said it, but the words carried a weight that hadn’t been present in any of their earlier exchanges. The sharp, defensive version of Masaomi had stepped back—just enough to let something more honest through.
Curtis kept pace beside him, long black braids shifting with the slower walk.
“I’m not going to use this against you either. So ease up.” A short pause, mismatched eyes glancing sideways. “So the ladies’ man is just a facade, huh? Or your old personality? Both, maybe?”
Masaomi let out a short, dry breath that was almost a laugh and almost not.
“…Both.” He didn’t bother dressing it up. “The flirty shit is easier. People expect it. It’s loud, it’s simple, and it keeps most of them from looking too close at anything else. Some of it’s real—I like women, I’m not going to pretend I don’t. But the version everyone sees at school is… lighter than what’s actually running underneath.”
He kicked a small piece of gravel along the sidewalk, watching it skitter ahead of them.
“The serious part is older. From before. From the stuff that put Saki in that window and made me swear Mikado wasn’t going to follow the same path.” A quiet beat. “You already clocked the gang history. So yeah. The carefree ladies’ man is partly a mask. Partly a leftover habit. Partly just me trying not to drag the heavier version into every room I walk into.”
He glanced at Curtis again, expression unreadable.
“You’re not exactly one-note yourself, Nightstar. Masked street version. School version. Whatever the hell that was on the sidewalk when you lost your mind over a missing photo. So don’t act like you’re only asking from the outside.”
Curtis let out a short sigh, the sound more resigned than annoyed.
“You were there too when my hoodie fell down.”
Masaomi actually laughed—short, genuine, and the first real one he had given since the hospital window.
“Yeah. When Mikado first got into the city, we were passing by.” He shook his head, a crooked grin pulling at the corner of his mouth. “Whole crowd stopped for this masked guy with a guitar, and then the hood drops and suddenly there’s braids and a whole different silhouette. Mikado wouldn’t shut up about it for like two blocks. Walker and Erika were already writing fanfiction in their heads.”
He glanced sideways at Curtis, the earlier heaviness thinned by the shared memory.
“Didn’t think the same guy would end up in our class, cracking concrete, telling off teachers, and casually diagnosing my entire personality on a sidewalk. Small damn city.”
The laugh faded into something quieter, but the edge between them had softened another degree. For the first time since the trash can and the punch, the walk felt less like two people measuring each other and more like two people who had simply ended up on the same street.
Curtis reached up and idly tugged one of the long black braids forward over his shoulder, mismatched eyes half-lidded.
“My braids are a dead giveaway most of the time. I can wear a mask, but from what I’ve seen around here… not many people with braids.” A small, almost dismissive shrug. “Then again, it’s more from my culture anyways.”
Masaomi glanced at the braid, then back at the street ahead.
“…Yeah. You don’t see that style much in Ikebukuro. Stands out even without the height and the eyes.” He was quiet for a second, processing the cultural note without turning it into a big interrogation. “Explains why people clock you so fast once the hood comes down. Mikado recognized you the next morning at school almost immediately. Anri too, apparently.”
A short, dry breath.
“For someone who clearly knows how to disappear when he wants to, you picked a look that’s pretty hard to lose in a crowd. Intentional, or just not worth changing?”
Curtis’s expression didn’t change, but his voice settled into something quieter and more deliberate.
“It’s from my culture.”
He let the braid rest against his chest again, mismatched eyes fixed ahead.
“There was a time white governments made it illegal for us to practice our own culture. Language, ceremonies, the way we wore our hair—all of it. Kids got their braids cut off in the schools. Parents could be punished for teaching the old ways. That system only really started breaking down recently, in the bigger picture.”
A short pause. No anger in the delivery. Just fact.
“So the braid means more now. It’s not just hair. It’s resilience. Perseverance. Proof we didn’t disappear when they tried to erase us.”
He glanced sideways at Masaomi once, then back to the street.
“That’s why I keep them. Mask or no mask.”
Masaomi was silent for several steps.
The usual sharp replies and easy deflection didn’t come right away. He looked at the long black braids with a different weight than before, the information settling into place alongside everything else he had learned about the tall foreigner in the last two days.
“…Damn,” he said finally, voice lower. “That’s… heavier than I expected.”
He didn’t offer empty sympathy or try to relate it to something smaller. He simply absorbed it.
“Explains a lot about why you don’t hide them, even when they’d make you easier to track. Some things aren’t worth cutting off just to blend in.” A short, rough breath. “Respect for carrying that out in the open.”
Curtis kept his expression flat as they walked, but the thought settled quietly behind his eyes.
And for my parents trying to erase me.
The braids weren’t only about the culture that had survived outside attempts to cut it away. They were also about the two people who had decided he wasn’t worth keeping and left him in a forest to disappear. He carried both meanings in the same plaits and didn’t separate them.
He didn’t say any of that out loud.

The familiar van rolled up alongside them before either could speak again.
The side door slid open. Kadota leaned out first, one arm resting on the frame, expression as calm and assessing as ever. Behind him, Walker’s eyes immediately lit up with recognition. Erika was already half-leaning forward with her phone out. Saburo looked like he was bracing for whatever nonsense was about to start.
Kadota’s gaze moved from Masaomi to the tall transfer student with the long black braids and mismatched eyes.
“Masaomi.” A short nod, then a direct look at Curtis. “Who’s the tall guy?”
Walker’s grin was already forming.
“No way. That’s the masked busker from the other night. The one who dropped the hood and then later rearranged half a street. I knew the braids looked familiar.”
Erika made a small, delighted sound.
“He’s even more protagonist-coded up close. The eyes are different colors. That’s illegal levels of character design.”
Saburo sighed from the driver’s seat.
“Can we not adopt strange powerful foreigners off the sidewalk for five minutes?”
Kadota ignored the commentary and kept his attention on Masaomi, waiting for an actual answer.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes moved from Kadota to the two in the back of the van. A small, genuine sound of amusement left him.
“Ohoho. You two are eccentric.” The corner of his mouth lifted slightly. “I like you both already.”
Walker looked like he had just been personally validated by the universe.
“See?! He gets it. The braids, the eyes, the mysterious foreigner energy—he understands the importance of strong character presence!”
Erika’s grin went wide and sharp with pure delight.
“Oh, I really like him. Most people tell us to shut up or look at us like we’re weird. He just accepted the eccentric label and said he likes it. That’s main-character behavior.” She leaned further toward the open door, already studying Curtis with open interest. “You’re the one from the street performance and the sidewalk incident. We’ve been theorizing about you for two days.”
Kadota exhaled through his nose, the long-suffering expression of a man who had accepted this exact dynamic years ago.
“Of course you have.”
Saburo muttered from the front,
“We’re never getting a normal afternoon again, are we.”
Masaomi pinched the bridge of his nose, but there was the faintest hint of reluctant amusement under the exasperation.
“Nightstar, this is the van gang. Kadota, Walker, Erika, Saburo. They’re… a lot. But they’re solid.” He glanced at Curtis. “You just made two of them extremely happy. Hope you’re ready for that.”
Curtis raised an eyebrow behind the long black braids, the small smile still faintly present.
“You both realize I’m normal looking, with the exception of my eyes. Where I’m from, right?”
Walker looked personally offended on behalf of Curtis’s entire silhouette.
“Normal?! You have heterochromia, cultural braids that most people in this city have never seen up close, the height, the whole quiet-dangerous energy—and you’re standing there calling it normal?”
Erika nodded vigorously, already typing something on her phone.
“He’s got protagonist-of-a-dark-urban-fantasy energy and he’s gaslighting us about being average. This is amazing. Where are you from that this is the baseline?”
Kadota glanced at Masaomi like this was entirely his fault for letting the conversation happen on the sidewalk.
Masaomi sighed.
“Northwest Territories. Canada. He mentioned it earlier.” He looked at Curtis. “They’re not going to let the ‘normal’ claim go, just so you know. Once Walker and Erika decide someone is main-character material, it’s permanent.”
Saburo leaned on the steering wheel.
“You should’ve kept walking. Now you’re stuck.”
The van gang piled out near the familiar sign of Russia Sushi, the usual post-school drift into the restaurant already in motion. Simon’s large silhouette was visible through the window as they approached. Curtis fell into step with them without comment, long black braids shifting against the blue uniform jacket as they stepped inside.
The bell above the door gave its usual soft ring.
Curtis glanced sideways at Walker and Erika as they claimed a table large enough for the group, mismatched eyes half-lidded with mild curiosity.
“So you guys like Chainsaw Man?”
The reaction was immediate.
Walker’s entire posture changed like someone had just plugged him into a power source.
“Do we like Chainsaw Man?! The public safety devil contracts, the fiend designs, the way Fujimoto weaponizes emotional devastation—don’t even get me started on the hybrid forms and the sheer commitment to psychological horror inside a shonen framework—”
Erika was already leaning across the table, eyes bright.
“The control devil arc alone should be taught in schools. And the way they handle Denji’s character is actually tragic when you stop laughing at the surface-level chaos. Also the devil designs are criminal levels of good. Which version are you on—manga or anime?”
Kadota sat down with the exhausted expression of a man who had watched this exact explosion happen dozens of times.
Saburo muttered,
“You had to ask.”
Masaomi dropped into the seat beside Curtis, already resigned.
“You just activated them. There’s no off switch once Chainsaw Man is on the table. Hope you actually meant the question.”
Simon appeared at the side of the table with his usual broad, warm presence, flyers still tucked under one arm, and gave Curtis a look of mild recognition mixed with approval that the tall foreigner had somehow ended up with this particular group.
Curtis leaned back slightly in his chair, long black braids resting against the blue uniform jacket as he answered without any particular fanfare.
“Both. I completed the manga. Saw the Reze movie in the States last year. Just waiting for everything else to be animated.”
Walker made a sound that was halfway between a gasp and a victory cry.
“He finished the manga. He saw Reze in theaters. This man is cultured. Erika, we have a problem—he’s actually ahead of us on the movie.”
Erika’s eyes went wide with pure delight and immediate interrogation mode.
“You saw Reze on the big screen? How was the audience reaction during the staircase sequence? Did they understand the tragedy or were they just loud the whole time? Also—manga complete means you’ve already suffered the aquarium arc and the falling devil. You poor, enlightened soul.”
She leaned further across the table.
“Opinion on the Control Devil’s writing. No wrong answers, but there are absolutely wrong answers.”
Kadota ordered for the table with the weary efficiency of someone who knew the food needed to arrive before the conversation became a full panel analysis. Saburo just shook his head and muttered something about “another one.”
Masaomi glanced at Curtis with something close to reluctant sympathy.
“You did this to yourself. They’re going to ask you about every arc in order now.”
Simon appeared with the first round of sushi and miso, setting the plates down with a broad, approving nod at the growing energy around the table. He gave Curtis a look that clearly translated to you fit with these strange ones better than expected, then moved back toward the counter without further comment.
Curtis sat in the middle of the sudden storm of otaku enthusiasm, mismatched eyes calm, and for the first time since arriving in Ikebukuro looked like someone who might actually stay at the table for a while.
Curtis shrugged, accepting another piece of sushi without urgency.
“It’s fine. I’m a fan of the work. Anyways—we can talk manga and anime later.” His mismatched eyes shifted to Masaomi, calm but direct. “You wouldn’t have me tag along with you today if you just wanted to hang. You’ve got Mikado for that.”
The table quieted half a degree.
Masaomi didn’t deny it. He set his chopsticks down, the earlier lighter mood thinning back into something more practical.
“…Yeah.” He looked around at Kadota, Walker, Erika, and Saburo. “I need advice. Mikado’s getting curious about the Dollars. He already tried floating the idea of using them to look for Mika and Seiji. I shut it down, but he’s not going to stop thinking about it. I need ways to keep him from actually joining—or getting pulled in deep enough that he can’t walk back out.”
Kadota’s expression went serious in the way it always did when the conversation turned to the real shape of the city.
“The Dollars aren’t a club. Once you’re in, the city’s problems start finding you whether you asked for them or not.”
Walker and Erika exchanged a look, the otaku energy cooling into something more grounded. Saburo leaned back in his chair with a heavy exhale.
“Kid’s soft,” Saburo said. “Not weak—soft. There’s a difference. Soft gets eaten out here.”
Masaomi nodded once, jaw tight, and waited for whatever practical suggestions the group could actually give him.

Elsewhere, on a quieter residential stretch, Mikado walked alone with his thoughts still tangled around Curtis, Anri, the Dollars, and the feeling of being left behind at the school gates.
That was when he saw him.
Seiji Yagiri.
Mikado’s steps slowed. The same sharp-faced boy who had walked out of their classroom on the first day was moving down the sidewalk ahead of him, and beside him walked a girl. For a brief, hopeful second Mikado’s mind jumped to Mika—
—then the details resolved.
The height was wrong. The hair was wrong. The way she carried herself was wrong. It wasn’t Mika Harima.
Mikado stopped at the corner, watching the two figures continue down the street, the question of who the girl actually was settling cold and unfinished in his chest. He didn’t call out. He simply stood there, the city’s late light stretching his shadow behind him, and realized the search for Mika had just become more complicated than a missing classmate.